WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
A pharmaceutical quality system,
often called a PQS, brings

2
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.800
together the people, processes and management
arrangements used to maintain medicine

3
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:14.500
quality. Good Manufacturing Practice
is a fundamental part of this system.

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.610
A strong quality system
defines responsibilities,

5
00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:21.020
establishes procedures,
monitors performance and

6
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:26.180
supports continual improvement. It does
not rely on final product testing alone.

7
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.700
Senior management is accountable for establishing
an effective pharmaceutical quality system

8
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.250
and providing suitable
resources. Responsibilities

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.520
and authorities should be clearly defined.

10
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.120
Quality units, production teams, engineering
and other functions must work together,

11
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.570
while preserving required
independent quality

12
00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:49.560
decisions. Management review
uses information about

13
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.590
performance, problems and improvement
opportunities to guide action.

14
00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:59.430
Quality risk management provides a structured
way to assess, control, communicate

15
00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:03.770
and review risks to product quality.
The level of formality, effort

16
00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:08.830
and documentation should reflect the risk
and available knowledge. Risk assessments

17
00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:13.830
are not a substitute for meeting
regulatory requirements. For example, the

18
00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:18.230
criticality of a temperature sensor
depends on its intended function and

19
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:20.060
potential effect on product quality.

20
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.880
When work departs from an approved
procedure or expected result, the

21
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.330
event should be documented
and evaluated through

22
00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.120
applicable quality
procedures. Investigation

23
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.660
depth should be proportionate to the
potential impact. Corrective and preventive

24
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.920
action, or CAPA, should
address identified causes where

25
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.970
appropriate. Effectiveness
checks help determine

26
00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.040
whether the action achieved its intended

27
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.680
outcome. Not every deviation requires
the same investigation or CAPA.

28
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.760
Changes to equipment, processes, software,
facilities or documents can affect validated

29
00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.980
status and product quality. A formal change
management process evaluates proposed

30
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.410
changes before implementation.
The assessment considers

31
00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:08.200
technical impact, regulatory
implications, validation

32
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.870
needs, training and
required approvals. After

33
00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.800
implementation, the
organization verifies that

34
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.270
the change was completed as intended and
that necessary evidence is available.

35
00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:24.750
Good documentation practices help ensure
that instructions are clear and records

36
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.950
accurately describe work
performed. An approved

37
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.170
standard operating procedure explains how

38
00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:34.750
to perform a defined activity. A
completed record provides evidence of

39
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:40.210
what actually happened. Entries should be
accurate, legible, attributable and made

40
00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:44.870
at the time of the activity, according
to applicable procedures. Corrections

41
00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:48.730
must preserve the original
information and remain traceable.

42
00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.110
Data integrity means that information is

43
00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.570
complete, consistent
and accurate throughout

44
00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:59.430
its lifecycle. The ALCOA
principles include attributable,

45
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:03.610
legible, contemporaneous,
original and accurate.

46
00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.090
Additional expectations
often described as A

47
00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:11.790
L C O A plus include complete,
consistent, enduring and available.

48
00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:17.470
These concepts apply to both paper and
electronic records. Access controls,

49
00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.930
audit trails and record retention should
be appropriate to the system and its risks.

50
00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:27.890
Consider a manufacturing vessel whose
critical instrument has an expired calibration

51
00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:32.930
status. The operator should not simply
ignore the finding. The equipment

52
00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:37.830
suitability and potential product quality
impact must be assessed under approved

53
00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:43.370
site procedures. The decision, rationale
and any necessary corrective action should

54
00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.710
be documented. This example connects the

55
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.630
pharmaceutical quality
system, risk management

56
00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:54.090
and good documentation practices
to a real operational decision.
